= Representativeness heuristic =

== Overview ==
The '''representativeness heuristic''' is the use of similarity to a prototype, category, or generating process as a cue in judgment.

== Judgment under uncertainty ==
A case that strongly resembles a category can be judged probable even when relevant base rates or sample-size information point elsewhere.

== Bias and useful inference ==
Similarity is often informative. The heuristic becomes misleading when representativeness is treated as if it were equivalent to probability.

== Related phenomena ==
Research on representativeness is closely connected to [[Base-rate neglect]], the [[Conjunction fallacy]], misconceptions of randomness, and sensitivity to sample size.

== References ==
* Daniel Kahneman & Amos Tversky, “Subjective Probability: A Judgment of Representativeness”, ''Cognitive Psychology'' 3 (1972): 430–454.
* Amos Tversky & Daniel Kahneman, “Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases”, ''Science'' 185 (1974): 1124–1131.

